ullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\pgfsys@beginscope\pgfsys@invoke{ }{{}}\color[rgb]{% 1,0,0}\definecolor[named]{pgfstrokecolor}{rgb}{1,0,0}\pgfsys@color@rgb@stroke{% 1}{0}{0}\pgfsys@invoke{ }\pgfsys@color@rgb@fill{1}{0}{0}\pgfsys@invoke{ }% \definecolor[named]{pgffillcolor}{rgb}{1,0,0}{ {}{{}}{} {}{}{{}}{}{{}}{}{}{} {}{}{{}}{}{{}}{}{}{} {}\pgfsys@moveto{0.0pt}{0.0pt}\pgfsys@lineto{1.13815pt}{0.0pt}\pgfsys@curveto{% 5.69046pt}{4.55229pt}{5.69046pt}{4.55229pt}{11.38092pt}{7.96661pt}% \pgfsys@lineto{10.24275pt}{7.96661pt}\pgfsys@curveto{5.69046pt}{5.69046pt}{5.6% 9046pt}{5.69046pt}{0.0pt}{1.13815pt}\pgfsys@closepath\pgfsys@fill% \pgfsys@invoke{ } {}{{}}{} {}{}{{}}{}{{}}{}{}{} {}{}{{}}{}{{}}{}{}{} {}\pgfsys@moveto{11.38092pt}{1.13815pt}\pgfsys@lineto{10.24275pt}{1.13815pt}% \pgfsys@curveto{5.69046pt}{3.4143pt}{5.69046pt}{3.4143pt}{0.0pt}{7.96661pt}% \pgfsys@lineto{1.13815pt}{7.96661pt}\pgfsys@curveto{5.69046pt}{4.55229pt}{5.69% 046pt}{4.55229pt}{11.38092pt}{2.27614pt}\pgfsys@closepath\pgfsys@fill% \pgfsys@invoke{ } } \pgfsys@invoke{ }\pgfsys@endscope{}{}{}\hss}\pgfsys@discardpath\pgfsys@invoke{% }\pgfsys@endscope\hss}}\endpgfpicture}}\par The lengths of the two legs of a % right triangle can be found using the Pythagorean theorem, which states that % in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse ($c^{2}$) is % equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the legs ($a^{2}+b^{2}$). \par Given that c = 13, we can substitute this into the Pythagorean theorem to% find $a^{2}$ and $b^{2}$: \par$$a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}$$\par$$5a^{2}+12b^{2}=13^{2}$$\par$$5(a^{2}+b^{2})=13^% {2}$$\par$$5a^{2}+5b^{2}=13^{2}$$\par$$a^{2}+b^{2}=13^{2}/5$$\par$$a^{2}+b^{2}% =26$$\par Now, we can use the fact that the ratio of the lengths of the legs % is 5:12 to find the actual values of a and b. Since 5a/12 = 1, we can set up % an equation: \par$$5a=12b$$\par We can simplify this equation by dividing both sides by 5: \par$$a=2b$$\par Now, we can use the fact that $a^{2}+b^{2}=26$ to find the % value of b: \par$$a^{2}+(2b)^{2}=26$$\par$$2a^{2}+4b^{2}=26$$\par$$b^{2}=13$$\par$$b=\sqrt% {13}$$\par Finally, we can use the equation 5a = 12b to find the value of a: \par$$5a=12(\sqrt{13})$$\par$$a=\sqrt{13}/5$$\par Therefore, the lengths of % the two legs of the right triangle are $\sqrt{13}/5$ and $\sqrt{13}/5$.\ \par{ f\#\#\# ChatGPT}: \hbox to11.38pt{\vbox to7.97pt{\pgfpicture% \makeatletter\hbox{	hinspace\lower 0.0pt\hbox to0.0pt{\pgfsys@beginscope% \pgfsys@invoke{ }\definecolor[named]{pgfstrokecolor}{rgb}{0,0,0}% \pgfsys@color@gray@stroke{0}\pgfsys@invoke{ }\pgfsys@color@gray@fill{0}% \pgfsys@invoke{ }\pgfsys@setlinewidth{	he\pgflinewidth}\pgfsys@invoke{ }% 
